+# Maintenance mode sample
+
+Demonstrates how to take individual parts of an API "offline" at runtime
+(e.g. during a slow data migration) without redeploying, and without
+building separate mechanisms for HTTP routes and WebSocket message handlers.
+
+## How it works
+
+- **`@AffectedByMaintenance()`** ([src/maintenance/affected-by-maintenance.decorator.ts](src/maintenance/affected-by-maintenance.decorator.ts))
+  is a `SetMetadata`-based decorator. Put it on an HTTP method, an entire
+  `@Controller()` class, or a gateway's `@SubscribeMessage()` handler - it's
+  just metadata, so the same decorator works everywhere.
+- **`MaintenanceModeService`** ([src/maintenance/maintenance-mode.service.ts](src/maintenance/maintenance-mode.service.ts))
+  is a singleton provider holding a boolean flag, toggled from inside the
+  running app via `enable()` / `disable()`.
+- **`MaintenanceModeGuard`** ([src/maintenance/maintenance-mode.guard.ts](src/maintenance/maintenance-mode.guard.ts))
+  reads that metadata with `Reflector` and consults `MaintenanceModeService`.
+  It's the exact same `CanActivate` for both transports - Nest's
+  `ExecutionContext` already abstracts over HTTP and WebSockets, so there's no
+  separate WS-specific mechanism. It only rejects calls that are both marked
+  *and* made while maintenance mode is on; everything else passes straight
+  through untouched. Register it globally (`APP_GUARD`, see
+  [src/maintenance/maintenance.module.ts](src/maintenance/maintenance.module.ts))
+  to cover every HTTP controller, and add `@UseGuards(MaintenanceModeGuard)`
+  on any gateway whose messages need the same protection (see
+  [src/events/events.gateway.ts](src/events/events.gateway.ts)).
+- **`MaintenanceAdminController`** ([src/maintenance/maintenance-admin.controller.ts](src/maintenance/maintenance-admin.controller.ts))
+  exposes `POST /internal/maintenance/enable` and `.../disable` so an
+  operator (or a script watching the migration) can flip the flag without a
+  new deployment.
+
+`AppController` and `MigrationController` show marking a single handler vs.
+an entire controller; `EventsGateway` shows the identical decorator on a
+WebSocket message handler (over Socket.IO, Nest's default WS transport).
+
+## Testing
+
+`src/maintenance/maintenance-mode.guard.spec.ts` unit-tests the guard
+directly: it builds a `Reflector`, a `MaintenanceModeService`, and a minimal
+hand-written `ExecutionContext` object, then asserts which handlers get
+rejected with maintenance mode on/off - no HTTP server or socket connection
+involved:
+
+```bash
+npm test
+```
+
+`e2e/app/app.e2e-spec.ts` and `e2e/events/events.e2e-spec.ts` exercise the
+same behavior end-to-end through a real (in-memory) HTTP server and a real
+WebSocket connection, toggling maintenance mode via the admin endpoint:
+
+```bash
+npm run test:e2e
+```
